# 🌐 Day 14 – Networking Fundamentals & Hands-on Checks

## 🎯 Goal

Learn basic networking concepts and practice common troubleshooting commands.

## 🧠 Quick Concepts

* **OSI Model:** 7 layers from Physical to Application.
* **TCP/IP Model:** Link → Internet → Transport → Application.
* **IP:** Internet layer — handles addressing and routing.
* **TCP/UDP:** Transport layer — handles communication between systems.
* **HTTP/HTTPS:** Application layer — used for web communication.
* **DNS:** Application layer — converts domain names into IP addresses.

Example:
`curl https://example.com` → HTTP/HTTPS → TCP → IP

## 🔧 Hands-on Checks

| Check        | Command                      | Observation                           |
| ------------ | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Identity     | `hostname -I`                | Displays the system IP address.       |
| Reachability | `ping google.com`            | Checks connectivity and latency.      |
| Path         | `traceroute google.com`      | Shows the network path to the target. |
| Ports        | `ss -tulpn`                  | Shows listening ports and services.   |
| DNS          | `dig google.com`             | Displays the resolved IP address.     |
| HTTP         | `curl -I https://google.com` | Shows the HTTP status code.           |
| Connections  | `netstat -an \| head`        | Shows active connections.             |

## 🔍 Port Probe

```bash
ss -tulpn
nc -zv localhost <port>
```

If the port is not reachable, I would check the **service status and firewall rules**.

## 💭 Reflection

* **Fastest check:** `ping` for basic connectivity.
* **DNS failure:** Check the **Application layer** and DNS configuration.
* **HTTP 500:** Check the application/server.
* **Follow-up checks:** Check `systemctl status` and firewall rules.

## 📌 Key Takeaway

I learned how to use basic networking commands to check **IP addresses, connectivity, DNS, ports, and HTTP services**.
